\chapter{The D Toolkit: Mixins, CTFE, and
Reflection}
\label{ch:d-toolkit}\label{c37-the-d-toolkit-mixins-ctfe-and-reflection}

\section{A port that tests the
abstraction}\label{c37-a-port-that-tests-the-abstraction}

The D implementation is valuable both as a usable toolkit and as a
comparison that reveals which ideas are independent of C++. Composition,
tree values, parser commitment, and equality saturation do not
intrinsically require CRTP, class-type non-type template parameters, or
\code{std::function}. D expresses their roles with template mixins,
native string arguments, alias callables, operator templates, and
compile-time function evaluation.

This chapter treats D as secondary without reducing it to a spelling
table. Some differences are merely syntax, such as a template argument
written with an exclamation mark. Others change behavior or cost. D's
expression nodes support indexed array evaluation; its PEG rules are
reference objects; its result type supports identical success and error
payloads; and its repetition parser checks for empty successful
iterations. A serious porting guide must identify these differences
explicitly.

\section{Composition without a recursive
base}\label{c37-composition-without-a-recursive-base}

\texttt{mixin\ DSL!(Features...)} injects declarations into the
enclosing aggregate. The built-in feature tags are empty structs, and
the mixin uses static tests of the selected tag list to inject
convenience members. It also records the list as \code{DSLFeatures},
rejects duplicate tags, and invokes a nested \code{Mixin} template on
custom feature tags when one exists.

This differs from the C++ arrangement where every built-in feature has a
nested mixin and \code{DSL} inherits all selected specializations. In D,
the custom-feature hook and the built-in static branches are distinct
mechanisms. The resulting host can still be queried with
\code{HasFeature}, but the query examines the recorded feature list
rather than a C++ base-class relation.

\section{Compile-time values and callable
binding}\label{c37-compile-time-values-and-callable-binding}

D permits strings directly as template arguments, so most APIs do not
need the optional \code{FixedString} compatibility wrapper.
\texttt{leaf!"number"} and \texttt{StaticRegex!"{[}0-9{]}+"} express
their fixed vocabulary directly. CTFE evaluates ordinary eligible
functions when a compile-time value is required. An \code{enum} manifest
constant is a common way to request such evaluation.

Generic lambdas are naturally passed as aliases when their parameter
types remain templated. \texttt{pipe!((x)\ =\textgreater{}\ x\ +\ x)()}
uses \code{StaticCallable} to present an invocable object without
runtime callable storage. Typed functions, delegates, and stateful
callable structs can instead be passed as ordinary values. Choose the
form according to the callable's type and ownership rather than assuming
every lambda follows one universal syntax.

Predicate negation uses \texttt{\textasciitilde{}p} or
\texttt{p.negated()} because D's logical negation operator produces a
Boolean rather than a new composable predicate. Parser repetition uses
\texttt{p.many()} or \texttt{many(p)} because unary star is not an
overloadable struct operator in the needed way. These are deliberate
language adaptations that preserve a role while changing its surface
expression.

\section{Ownership differences that affect
programs}\label{c37-ownership-differences-that-affect-programs}

The D AST is a struct with explicitly duplicated child storage on
copying, so modifying a copied tree's descendant does not modify the
original tree. That is a stronger contract than a default shallow copy
of a dynamic array would provide. The embedded tests exercise nested
copies, which is important because duplicating only the outermost array
would not establish deep value semantics for descendants.

PEG rules, definitions, and matchers are classes. Appending a new rule
keeps previous rule references pointing at the same allocated objects.
This avoids the C++ vector-element address invalidation problem, though
it does not make all mutable PEG state thread safe. Directed graphs and
e-graphs are also reference objects in D; assigning their references
aliases state rather than creating an independent speculative copy.

\section{Results, repetition, and indexed
evaluation}\label{c37-results-repetition-and-indexed-evaluation}

The D result type stores a success discriminator and separate payload
fields. Tagged \code{OkWrap} and \code{ErrWrap} constructors
disambiguate identical payload types. \texttt{Result!(string,string)}
can therefore be queried and extracted correctly, which makes the D task
pipeline a working realization of the same high-level design that is
blocked by the C++ snapshot's duplicate-type variant queries.

The D repetition parser checks whether a successful child advanced the
cursor. If not, it reports a committed nonempty-repetition error instead
of looping forever. This does not make a zero-width repeated grammar
sensible; it makes the failure observable. Portable grammars should
still establish progress as an invariant rather than depend on a
language-specific diagnostic.

Indexed expression nodes expose a length and element evaluation
operation. A combined array expression can compute each output element
from its operands without materializing every intermediate array. Length
mismatch is checked. This mechanism is absent from the C++
\code{BinExpr::eval}, which evaluates whole operand values and invokes
their operators. Similar expression syntax therefore carries different
fusion capabilities in the two files.

\section{Native enum reflection}\label{c37-native-enum-reflection}

D's reflection implementation enumerates declared members through
language traits. It removes duplicate values according to its policy,
orders reflected values, and supports user-defined attributes for flags
intent and display names. There is no default minus-128-to-127 discovery
interval analogous to the embedded C++ implementation. Large sparse
enumerator values can be discovered without scanning every intervening
integer.

That convenience does not replace application compatibility policy.
Renaming a member still changes an uncustomized external name; changing
an explicit numeric value still changes a numeric wire representation.
Native reflection improves how the table is obtained, not what
guarantees a persisted format should make. The same boundary design
principles from Chapter 7 apply.

\section{A verified importing
program}\label{c37-a-verified-importing-program}

\code{projects/d-overview.d} imports the module rather than relying only
on tests inside it. It exercises a mixed-feature host, an alias-bound
pipe, deep AST copying, a same-type result, sparse enum reflection, and
equality saturation. The book's \code{make d-check} command builds and
runs it with LDC. Its output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Notation, algorithm, or output}]
10 (add (number 1) (number 2)) a
\end{lstlisting}

The complete importing program is included from its maintained source:

\lstinputlisting[style=dsld,caption={D companion: importing the module and checking language-specific contracts.},label={lst:d-companion}]{projects/d-overview.d}

The embedded module tests provide broader coverage, while an external
import checks visibility and real consumer usage. Keep those forms of
evidence separate. A module can compile its own private helper tests yet
expose a problem to an importing client, and a tiny client can pass
without exercising every embedded algorithm. Together they provide a
more useful verification picture than either one alone.

The reference guide that follows maps the established feature families
to D. The later shared subsystems--S-expressions, e-graphs, codecs, I/O,
visitors, static byte regexes, preprocessing, CFGs, graphs, terms, and
reduction--also exist in the module. Their earlier chapters explain the
algorithms primarily through C++; this chapter supplies the ownership
and language mechanisms needed to carry those explanations over
accurately.

\code{DSLtk.d} is a self-contained D module using Phobos. This chapter
maps the established C++ toolkit to D and introduces the
language-specific API. The shared facilities introduced in Chapter 3
have focused tutorials in Chapters 32--36.

\section{Build and import}\label{c37-build-and-import}

Keep \code{DSLtk.d} on the import path and supply the module to the
compiler:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Notation, algorithm, or output}]
# From the DSLtk directory, with the user's app.d alongside the module:
ldc2 app.d DSLtk.d -of=app
dmd app.d DSLtk.d -of=app
gdc app.d DSLtk.d -o app
\end{lstlisting}

For an application in another directory, add the module's directory to
\code{-I}. The CMake installation includes the D source module. Its C++
\code{INTERFACE} target exports C++ usage requirements; D compilation is
driven by the D compiler invocation above.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
import DSLtk;
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Compose features with a
mixin}\label{c37-compose-features-with-a-mixin}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
struct MathDSL
{
    mixin DSL!(Pipeline, Operators, AST, ExprTemplates);
    double val;
    double expr_value() const { return val; }
}
\end{lstlisting}

The mixin adds selected methods directly to the enclosing aggregate.
Features must be unique. \code{DSLFeatures} records the selected types;
\texttt{HasFeature!(T,\ F)} queries that list. The convenience
predicates are \texttt{Pipeable!T}, \texttt{ExprLike!T},
\texttt{HasLiterals!T}, and \texttt{Rewritable!T}.

