Other built-in tags record feature presence while their facilities are
used through module-level functions/types. Their list includes
\code{PatternMatch}, \code{LazyFeature}, \code{Monadic},
\code{ResultFeature}, \code{CombinatorParser}, \code{TaskPipeline},
\code{EnumReflection}, and the later shared facility tags catalogued in
Chapter 3.

A custom feature can provide a nested mixin:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
struct DomainFeature
{
    mixin template Mixin()
    {
        int domain_value() const { return 17; }
    }
}
struct DomainDSL { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, DomainFeature); }
static assert(HasFeature!(DomainDSL, DomainFeature));
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Callable values and alias
callables}\label{c37-callable-values-and-alias-callables}

Typed functions, delegates and functors can be passed as ordinary
values:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
auto result = 5 | pipe((int x) => x * 2) | pipe((int x) => x + 1);
assert(result == 11);
\end{lstlisting}

Generic lambdas can instead be passed as template aliases:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
auto doubleIt = pipe!((x) => x + x)();
assert((4 | doubleIt) == 8);
auto positive = predicate!((x) => x > 0)();
assert(positive(2) && positive(2.5));
\end{lstlisting}

\texttt{StaticCallable!fn} binds an alias callable to a concrete
functor. Alias factories include \texttt{pipe!fn()},
\texttt{predicate!fn()}, \texttt{when!(key,\ fn)()},
\texttt{otherwise!fn()}, \texttt{on!(tag,\ fn)()}, and
\texttt{enum\_switch!fn(value)}.

\section{Pipelines, predicates and
dispatch}\label{c37-pipelines-predicates-and-dispatch}

Predicates compose through \texttt{\&} and \texttt{\textbar{}} with
short-circuit evaluation. Use \texttt{\textasciitilde{}} or
\texttt{negated()} for predicate negation; D's \texttt{!} produces a
boolean.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
auto positive = predicate((int x) => x > 0);
auto even = predicate((int x) => x % 2 == 0);
auto valid = positive & even;
assert(valid(4) && !valid(3));
assert((~positive)(-2));
\end{lstlisting}

Dispatch handlers receive the key, followed by any additional arguments:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
auto table = match(
    when!1((int key) => "one"),
    otherwise((int key) => "other"));
assert(table(1) == "one");

auto classify = match(
    when!(pattern!"[0-9]+")((string key) => "number"),
    otherwise((string key) => "other"));
assert(classify("123") == "number");
\end{lstlisting}

Handlers must have a common non-void result type. An unmatched key
without an \code{otherwise} clause raises an exception.
\texttt{pattern!S} is the small whole-input matcher for literals, dot,
classes/ranges, anchors and \texttt{*}/\texttt{+}; the broader byte-NFA
API is \texttt{StaticRegex!S} in book Chapter 32.

\section{Value-type ASTs and bottom-up
rewriting}\label{c37-value-type-asts-and-bottom-up-rewriting}

String tags are native template parameters:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
auto tree = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(1), leaf!"num"(2));
assert(tree.dump() == "(add (num 1) (num 2))");
auto copy = tree;
copy.child(0) = leaf!"num"(10);
assert(tree.child(0).value() == "1");
\end{lstlisting}

\code{ASTNode} deeply copies mutable child storage. Its accessors are
\code{tag}, \code{is_leaf}, \code{value}, \code{children}, \code{child},
\code{size}, \code{dump}, and \code{toString}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
import std.conv : to;
auto rules = rewrite_set(rule!"fold-add"(
    (ASTNode n) => n.tag() == "add" && n.size() == 2 &&
                  n.child(0).is_leaf() && n.child(1).is_leaf(),
    (ASTNode n) => leaf!"num"(
        to!int(n.child(0).value()) + to!int(n.child(1).value()))));
assert(rules.apply(tree).dump() == "(num 3)");
\end{lstlisting}

Rules run bottom-up and in registration order. \code{max_iterations}
defaults to 100; passes stop when the tree no longer changes. Equality
saturation in Chapter 29 retains multiple equivalent expressions rather
than replacing the current tree on each rule application.

\section{Lazy expressions, literals and
caches}\label{c37-lazy-expressions-literals-and-caches}

\texttt{terminal(value)} lifts a scalar or array into an expression
tree. Array expressions evaluate element by element into one result
array; participating arrays must have matching lengths. Scalars can
participate in array operations.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
auto a = terminal([1, 2, 3]);
auto b = terminal([4, 5, 6]);
auto c = terminal([7, 8, 9]);
assert((a + b * c).eval() == [29, 42, 57]);
assert((2 * a).eval() == [2, 4, 6]);
\end{lstlisting}

Terminal arrays use D's slice storage semantics. Copying a terminal does
not deep-copy the referenced array. \code{ASTNode} explicitly supplies
deep-copy behavior for its child tree.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
auto units = literal_set(
    lit!"_km"((real n) => n * 1000), lit!"_m"((real n) => n));
assert(units.parse("3.5_km") == 3500);
enum distance = literal_set(lit!"_km"((real n) => n * 1000)).parse("3.5_km");
static assert(distance == 3500);
\end{lstlisting}

\texttt{memoize!(Key,\ Value)(fn)} caches results by associative-array
key and provides \code{clear_cache}. \texttt{make\_lazy(fn)} infers the
producer/result types; \texttt{Lazy!T(fn)} accepts a delegate/function
producer. \texttt{get()} and \texttt{force()} return the cached value by
reference. \texttt{is\_computed()} reports whether production has
occurred.

\section{Optional and result flows}\label{c37-optional-and-result-flows}

\texttt{Maybe!T} supplies \code{map}, \code{flat_map}, \code{filter},
\code{or_else}, \code{has_value}, \code{value}, and \code{as_optional}.
The module-level optional helpers accept Phobos \texttt{Nullable!T}
values:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
import std.typecons : Nullable;
assert(Maybe!int(5).map((int n) => n + 1).or_else(0) == 6);
assert(map(Nullable!int(3), (int n) => n * 2).get == 6);
\end{lstlisting}

\texttt{Result!(T,\ E)} explicitly tags success/error branches,
including when \code{T} and \code{E} are identical. Use \code{from_ok},
\code{from_err}, or \code{Ok}/\code{Err} wrappers. Operations are
\code{is_ok}, \code{is_err}, \code{unwrap}, \code{unwrap_err},
\code{unwrap_or}, \code{map}, \code{and_then}, and \code{map_err}.
Incorrect unwrap operations raise an exception.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
alias R = Result!(string, string);
auto ok = R.from_ok("value");
auto err = R.from_err("error");
assert(ok.map((string s) => s.length).unwrap() == 5);
assert(err.map((string s) => s.length).unwrap_err() == "error");
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Parsers, semantic actions and
PEG}\label{c37-parsers-semantic-actions-and-peg}

\texttt{parser(fn)} wraps a callable taking \code{ref ParsecInput} and
returning \texttt{ExpectedResult!T}. Composition preserves concrete
parser/callable types:

{\def\LTcaptype{none} % do not increment counter
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
Operation & D syntax \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\endlastfoot
Sequence & \texttt{a\ \&\ b} returns a \code{Tuple} \\
Choice & \texttt{a\ \textbar{}\ b} with equal output types \\
Zero-or-more & \texttt{p.many()} / \texttt{many(p)} \\
Optional & \texttt{p.optional()} / \texttt{optional(p)} \\
Backtracking & \texttt{try\_parse(p)} \\
Expected-label replacement & \texttt{labeled(p,\ label)} \\
Whole-source execution & \texttt{run\_parser(p,\ source)} \\
\end{longtable}
}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
auto pair = ch('a') & ch('b');
auto alternate = try_parse(pair) | (ch('a') & ch('c'));
assert(!run_parser(alternate, "ac").value.isNull);
auto digits = satisfy((char c) => c >= '0' && c <= '9', "digit").many();
assert(run_parser(digits, "123").value.get == ['1', '2', '3']);
\end{lstlisting}

Inspect \code{value.isNull} and \code{error} on a \code{ParseOutcome}.
Failures distinguish \code{Soft} and \code{Committed}; \code{try_parse}
restores the cursor and softens a failure. Repetition rejects successful
parsers that consume no input. \code{parse_with_action} passes a
\code{ParserStage} span to a user callback; \texttt{production!id(fn)}
stores a compile-time production identifier with its handler.

PEG combinators are \code{peg_seq}, \code{peg_choice}, \code{peg_opt},
\code{peg_many}, \code{peg_many1}, \code{peg_and}, \code{peg_not},
\code{peg_rule}, and \code{peg_channel}.
\texttt{create\_peg\_definition()} and
\texttt{derive\_peg\_definition(parent)} return reference-type
definitions. \texttt{add\_rule!pattern(action)} returns a stable rule
reference. Channels, semantic actions and fluent matcher arms follow
Chapters 27--28, using D callbacks such as
\texttt{(ref\ PEGMatch\ m)\ \{\ ...\ \}}. PEG patterns retain their
documented greedy prefix semantics.

\section{Tasks and shared state}\label{c37-tasks-and-shared-state}

\texttt{Task(name,\ callback)} takes a \code{ref TaskState} callback
returning \texttt{Result!(string,\ string)}.
\texttt{task1\ \textbar{}\ task2} creates a \code{TaskChain}; \code{run}
records each result by task name. Policies are \code{StopOnError} and
\code{ContinueOnError}. \texttt{suspend(state)} stops subsequent work;
\code{upon} runs a task when a supplied event callable returns true.
Stateful caches, lazy values and PEG objects do not provide
synchronization.

\section{Native enum reflection}\label{c37-native-enum-reflection-1}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
enum Color { Red = -1000, Green = 0, AliasGreen = 0, Blue = 1000 }
static assert(enum_count!Color() == 3);
static assert(enum_name!(Color.Red)() == "Red");
static assert(enum_values!Color() == [Color.Red, Color.Green, Color.Blue]);
auto blue = enum_cast!Color("blue", case_insensitive);
assert(blue.get == Color.Blue);
auto name = enum_switch!((c) => enum_name!(typeof(c).value)())(Color.Blue);
assert(name == "Blue");
\end{lstlisting}

Declared members are reflected without numeric search-range limits.
Values are sorted numerically and duplicate values use the first
declared name. Checked casts/indexing and adjacency return
\code{Nullable}; arrays of values, names and entries are returned as
fixed-size values. \texttt{enum\_reflected!E(integer)} checks reflected
membership in D. \code{enum_switch} has an optional fallback; its
no-fallback form raises an exception for an unknown value.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsld,caption={Source or application fragment}]
@(EnumOptions(true)) enum Access { Read = 1, Write = 2, Execute = 4, All = 7 }
assert(enum_flags_name(Access.Read | Access.Write) == "Read|Write");
@(EnumOptions(false, 2)) @(EnumNames(["c_Red": "rouge"]))
enum Named { c_Red, c_Blue }
static assert(enum_name(Named.c_Red) == "rouge");
static assert(enum_name(Named.c_Blue) == "Blue");
\end{lstlisting}

\code{EnumOptions} sets flags mode and prefix trimming; \code{EnumNames}
maps declaration names to display names. Flags reflection selects
single-bit members. Helpers include \code{enum_flags_cast},
\code{enum_flags_contains}, \code{enum_flags_test}, and
\code{enum_flags_test_any}. \texttt{EnumArray!(E,\ V)},
\texttt{EnumBitset!E}, and \texttt{EnumSet!E} supply enum-indexed
containers. D's native enum formatting/bitwise operators supply the
host-language integrations. Chapter 7 documents C++'s upstream-specific
configuration, namespace aliases and stream/formatter APIs.

